\details{
\subsection{Identifying clades}{

Clades can be defined by tips, a node ID, or a name:
\itemize{
\item A pair of tips defines the clade as their MRCA node (e.g., \code{"t26, t18"}), separated by commas. A single tip defines the "clade" as the terminal branch of this tip.
\strong{Tip labels must include at least one other character than digits.}
\item A node ID of \code{phy} (digits only, e.g., \code{"34"}) also defines the clade, as in \code{\link[=build_missing_taxa_list]{phylograftR::build_missing_taxa_list()}}.
\item A single value that is not a tip of \code{phy} is a \strong{clade name} (e.g., \code{"Family_A"}). Names are matched with the names of the clades of \code{missing_taxa_list}
(\verb{$Target_clade_name}, \verb{$Sister_clade_name}, and the names of the excluded subclades) and, for lists built from taxonomic tables, with all the taxonomic groups of the backbone taxonomy.
The taxonomic groups are stored in the attribute \code{"taxonomic_groups"} of the object returned by \code{\link[=build_missing_taxa_list_from_taxonomy]{phylograftR::build_missing_taxa_list_from_taxonomy()}}:
they are only available if \code{missing_taxa_list} is this object itself, not one of its elements (\verb{$taxa} or \verb{$clades}) alone.
}

\strong{Clades are matched by their MRCA node in \code{phy}}: a clade can only be listed once, and
the tips written in the output are those already used in \code{missing_taxa_list} for the same clade, so the tips match in all the objects.
The grafting functions require \strong{all the clades used in \code{missing_taxa_list} to be listed}:
the clades that are not in the table are added with an unknown richness (\code{NA}), and their number is reported.
}

\subsection{Unsampled species}{

For a focal clade with a \code{total_richness} value provided: \code{total_richness = nb_backbone + nb_missing + nb_unsampled},
with \code{nb_missing} the number of lineages to graft within the focal clade, and \code{nb_unsampled} the number of species in this clade that are not represented in the backbone, nor will be grafted,
but must be accounted in the sampling fraction for when fitting diversification models.

For a clade with no \code{total_richness} value (\code{NA} or \code{NULL}), \code{nb_unsampled} are estimated as the sum of \code{nb_unsampled} over the largest clades nested within it with a \code{total_richness} value provided.
If there is none, \code{nb_unsampled = 0}. The rationale is that even if \code{nb_unsampled} cannot be derived from its \code{total_richness}
(as it is not provided for this clade), it has to at least account for the \code{nb_unsampled} species that have being identified in its own nested clades.
}

\subsection{Full sequence of grafting}{

The same table is valid for the grafting of the taxa within target clades and for the grafting of the missing clades as sister to backbone clades, in either order.
With the recommended sequence (taxa first, then missing clades on the grafted phylogeny):
\itemize{
\item When the taxa are grafted, the taxa to graft as missing clades are not in the backbone nor in the list, so they are counted among the unsampled species of the clades.
\item When the missing clades are grafted, the taxa grafted before are tips of the backbone, and the unsampled species are the species still unaccounted for.
}

The function checks the richness against all the taxa to graft at once: \strong{the function stops if the total richness of a clade is lower than the number of species it contains}
(backbone species and all the taxa to graft within it, or within clades nested within it),
or if a clade has fewer unsampled species than the clades with a total richness nested within it.
}

\subsection{Richness table}{

With \code{return_richness_table = TRUE}, the output has the attribute \code{"richness_table"}: a data.frame that reports, \strong{before any grafting},
the richness and the sampling fraction expected in each clade (see Value for details on its columns). It can be used to \strong{examine a priori the sampling fraction of the clades}
and to spot errors in the total richness: e.g., an unlikely low sampling fraction, or clades with an unknown richness (\code{NA}) for which unsampled species were derived from nested clades, or assumed to be none.
It can also be used to compare the sampling of different clades, or to decide which clades need a better estimate of their richness.

\strong{The sampling fractions of the table are expectations, and the effective sampling fractions of the grafted phylogenies may differ.}
The table assumes that all the taxa to graft end up in the clade where they belong: a taxon counts for all the clades that are, or enclose, its target clade,
and a missing clade counts for the clades that enclose its sister clade (it is grafted on the stem of the sister clade, so not within the sister clade itself).
However, where taxa are actually grafted varies among the grafted phylogenies (and among the stochastic maps, if any):
a taxon can be grafted on the stem of its target clade, which is outside of the clade, or within a clade nested in its target clade, which changes the richness of these clades.
The actual effective richness and sampling fraction of each clade, in each grafted phylogeny, are extracted \strong{a posteriori} with \code{\link[=extract_sampling_info]{phylograftR::extract_sampling_info()}}
on the output of the grafting functions, using the same \code{total_richness_per_clades}.
}
}
\examples{
# Load the backbone, the taxonomic tables, and the toy lists of taxa and missing clades
data(toy_backbone)
data(toy_backbone_taxonomic_table)
data(toy_missing_taxa_taxonomic_table)

# Build the missing taxa list for both types of missing lineages (taxa and clades)
lists <- build_missing_taxa_list_from_taxonomy(
  backbone_taxonomic_table = toy_backbone_taxonomic_table,
  missing_taxa_taxonomic_table = toy_missing_taxa_taxonomic_table,
  phy = toy_backbone, verbose = FALSE)
names(lists)

# Inform the total richness of some clades, written with their taxonomic names.
# Use NA if the total richness is unknown
richness_table <- data.frame(Clade = c("Family_A", "Family_B", "Subfam_B1", "Tribe_B2"),
                             total_richness = c(25, NA, 14, 8))

# Use the the function to validate this richness table against the backbone tree and missing taxon lists
richness_per_clades <- build_total_richness_per_clades(data = richness_table, phy = toy_backbone, missing_taxa_list = lists)

# Output = one list for each grafting function, with the clades of both lists and the same tips as in 'lists'
str(richness_per_clades, 2)

# Expected richness and sampling fraction of each clade, examined before any grafting
attr(richness_per_clades, "richness_table")

# Use the lists to build the fully grafted phylogenies in the recommended sequence:
# the missing taxa first, and then the missing clades
grafted_taxa <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = toy_backbone, missing_taxa_list = lists$taxa,
                                   total_richness_per_clades = richness_per_clades$taxa,
                                   seed = 1234, verbose = FALSE)
grafted_phy <- graft_missing_clades(phy = grafted_taxa, missing_taxa_list = lists$clades,
                                    total_richness_per_clades = richness_per_clades$clades,
                                    seed = 1234, verbose = FALSE)

